<section id="recognition">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-label">Recognition</div>
    <h2>Your team may already be naming the symptom wrong.</h2>
    <p class="section-copy">
      Design sees a copy problem. Product sees onboarding friction. Support sees repeated tickets.
      Engineering sees edge cases. Leadership sees activation, conversion, or data-quality risk.
    </p>
    <p class="section-copy">
      They may all be seeing the same failure from different angles: the product asks the user to
      continue before the next move has been made legible.
    </p>
    <div class="method-note">That is where the review begins.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="problem">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-label">Phenomenon</div>
    <h2>A clean interface can still leave the user stranded.</h2>
    <p class="section-copy">
      Most product teams inspect the screen. I inspect the moment where the user is expected to
      continue without enough sequence, context, language, trust, consequence-awareness or decision support.
    </p>
    <p class="section-copy">
      A flow breaks when the product asks for action before the next step is legible, safe, and consequentially clear.
    </p>
    <p class="section-copy">
      When that happens, users do not always complain. They guess, delay, abandon, submit bad data,
      or push the cost into support.
    </p>
    <div class="method-note">
      This is not a visual problem first.<br />
      It is a guidance failure.
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
